# Introduction # {#intro}

Sensor observations, positioning data, measurements, mobility information... are commonly published as time series: a timestamp with one or more values.
Representing time series in RDF drastically raises the verbosity, as each reading is often given its own identifier, for which contextual information is repeated on all readings.
With RDF Time Series Sources, a publisher keeps the readings compact and describes them with RDF:

- A [=Source=] describes a table of readings: every [=Point=] has one time and values for one or more [=Fields=]. Series that are measured together share one [=Time Field=].
- The class of a source says where its readings are: embedded in the metadata, as one small RDF node per point (`tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`) or as one JSON literal (`tss:EmbeddedJSONSource`), or kept in an external Parquet, CSV or Arrow data file (`tss:ParquetSource`, `tss:CSVSource`, `tss:ArrowSource`). The same field descriptions read them all.
- Each field states the datatype of its values and, for a data file or JSON points, its column name; optionally, it states what they observe, in which unit, with which sensor, of which feature of interest. In RDF points, the IRI of the field names its values, so it needs no column name.
- A data file can give the time of each row as a step on a regular [=Time Grid=], and state the values of a field as differences from a baseline (`tss:valueEncoding`).

The metadata also states the [=Time Window=] of the readings, whether readings may still be added, and an optional [=Stored Summary=] of each field. A consumer can follow a source to which readings are still added, and only fetch the new readings with HTTP Range requests ([[#following]]).

A source usually holds a whole time series, also when it grows large: it is not split into smaller parts for consumers to find. A consumer that only needs part of the time extent can still fetch less, with HTTP Range requests on the formats that allow it. The footer of a Parquet file says where each row group is and, usually, the smallest and largest value of each column in it, so a consumer can fetch the footer first and then only the row groups it needs. The footer of an Arrow file in the IPC file format likewise says where each record batch is.

## Namespaces ## {#namespaces}

RDF Time Series Sources use the following prefix and namespace:

```turtle
@prefix tss: <https://w3id.org/tss#> .
```

Note: Earlier drafts of this specification were called RDF Time Series Snippets. The namespace stays the same, but `tss:Source` was `tss:Snippet`, the five source classes, such as `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, ended in `Snippet`, such as `tss:EmbeddedRDFSnippet`, and `tss:sourceDescription` was `tss:snippetDescription`.

## Embedded example ## {#embedded-example}

This example is a complete [=Embedded Source=]: five conductivity readings of one sensor, embedded in the metadata as RDF points. Readings may still be added to it: it does not state `tss:immutable true`. It is therefore written as an RDF Message Log [[RDF-MESSAGES]]: the first message describes the source and its fields, and each next message holds one point. A writer adds a point by appending one message ([[#updating]]), and a consumer that follows the source only fetches the messages it has not read yet ([[#following]]).

<div class="example" id="example-embedded-source">
```turtle
VERSION "1.2-messages"
PREFIX tss: <https://w3id.org/tss#>
PREFIX rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
PREFIX quantitykind: <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/>
PREFIX unit: <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/>
PREFIX ex: <https://example.org/ex#>

ex:source
    a tss:EmbeddedRDFSource ;
    rdfs:label "Conductivity readings"@en ;
    tss:producedBy ex:producer ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:timeField ex:time-field ;
    tss:key ex:time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:time-field,
        ex:value-field .

ex:time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    rdfs:label "Observation time"@en ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTimeStamp .

ex:value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    rdfs:label "Electrical conductivity"@en ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:minimum "0"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:maximum "10000"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    tss:unit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM ;
    tss:sensor ex:sensor-1 ;
    tss:featureOfInterest ex:feature-1 .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:value-field "4810.5"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:value-field "4722.4"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T08:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:value-field "4751.9"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T09:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:value-field "4719.3"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T09:45:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:value-field "4783.6"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
```
</div>

The source is a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`: it links to each point with `tss:hasPoint`, one message per point, and the IRIs of the fields are the predicates of the point's values, so the fields state no column name. Every message ends with the delimiter `MESSAGE`, so a consumer knows that the last message it read is complete. Expanded into observations ([[#expanding]]), every value of a [=Measured Field=] becomes one observation, with a link back to the source, the time of its point and what its field says. Written with SOSA [[VOCAB-SSN]] and `dcterms:source` ([[#sosa-mapping]]), the first two observations are:

<div class="example" id="example-observations">
```turtle
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix qudt: <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/> .

[] a sosa:Observation ;
    dcterms:source ex:source ;
    sosa:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    sosa:madeBySensor ex:sensor-1 ;
    sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ex:feature-1 ;
    qudt:hasUnit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM ;
    sosa:resultTime "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    sosa:hasSimpleResult "4810.5"^^xsd:double .

[] a sosa:Observation ;
    dcterms:source ex:source ;
    sosa:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    sosa:madeBySensor ex:sensor-1 ;
    sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ex:feature-1 ;
    qudt:hasUnit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM ;
    sosa:resultTime "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    sosa:hasSimpleResult "4722.4"^^xsd:double .

# ... and three more observations, one for each other point
```
</div>

The examples that follow reuse the prefixes of these two examples.

# Definitions # {#definitions}

A <dfn>Time Series</dfn> is a set of data points ordered by time, where each data point consists of a timestamp and a value.

A <dfn>Source</dfn> is a table of readings that share one time field, described by one node of one of the five source classes ([[#storage]]): each row is a [=Point=], each column a [=Field=]. Each field other than the [=Time Field=] holds one [=Time Series=].

A <dfn>Field</dfn> is one column of a [=Source=], described by one `tss:Field`: the name and datatype of its values and, optionally, what they mean.

A <dfn>Point</dfn> is one row of a [=Source=]: one time, and at most one value for each [=Field=].

The <dfn>Time Field</dfn> of a source is the field that holds the time of each point (`tss:timeField`).

The <dfn>Key</dfn> of a source is the field whose values identify a point: its [=Time Field=] (`tss:key`).

The <dfn>Time Window</dfn> of a source is the period from `tss:from` (included) until `tss:until` (not included). A point lies in the window when its time is not before `tss:from` and before `tss:until`. A bound that is not stated leaves that side of the window open.

An <dfn>Embedded Source</dfn> keeps its points in the metadata: a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` as RDF nodes that it links to with `tss:hasPoint`, a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` as one JSON literal, `tss:points`. An <dfn>External Source</dfn> keeps them in a data file, `tss:dataFile`: a `tss:ParquetSource`, a `tss:CSVSource` or a `tss:ArrowSource`.

The <dfn id="dfn-time-grid">Time Grid</dfn> of an external source is a series of times at regular steps: step 0 at `tss:timeBase`, and every next step `tss:timeStep` later ([[#time-grid]]).

The <dfn>Step Field</dfn> of an external source with a time grid is the field whose column holds the step of each row (`tss:stepField`).

An <dfn>Empty Value</dfn> is the absence of a value: an empty CSV cell, a Parquet or Arrow null, an RDF point without a triple for the field, a JSON point without the field's key or with `null` for it, or the empty text `""`. A text that holds only spaces is not empty. Consequently, the empty string cannot be a value: an empty text and no value are the same thing.

Note: This is a known limitation: a field cannot hold the empty string as a value. A property that states which text means "no value" could lift it.

The <dfn>Decoded Value</dfn> of a value is what the value stands for: the value itself, or, for a field with a `tss:valueEncoding`, the value decoded as its encoding says ([[#value-encoding]]).

A <dfn>Measured Field</dfn> is a field with a `tss:observedProperty` that is not the time field. Each of its values is an observation.

A <dfn>Describing Field</dfn> says something about the values of another field (`tss:describesField`).

A <dfn>Stored Summary</dfn> holds numbers computed from the values of one field, such as their count and mean (`tss:summary`).

A <dfn>Growing Source</dfn> is a source that does not state `tss:immutable true`: points may still be added to it ([[#change]]).

A <dfn>Write</dfn> is a change to the points of a source: adding a point, since points are never changed or removed ([[#change]]).

# The source # {#the-source}

A metadata document MUST describe at least one source. Each source in the document is described and read on its own: it has its own fields, points and data file, and each field and each point belongs to exactly one source. A source has one time field, which gives the time of every point for all its other fields. The table lists the properties of a source. "Required" means exactly one value unless the table says otherwise, "optional" at most one, and "not allowed" none.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>External source</th><th>Embedded source</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:immutable</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>optional; default <code>false</code>; required with value <code>true</code> for a <code>tss:ParquetSource</code> and for a <code>tss:ArrowSource</code> in the Arrow IPC file format</td><td>optional; default <code>false</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasField</code></td><td>a <code>tss:Field</code></td><td>required, one or more</td><td>required, one or more</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeField</code></td><td>one of its fields</td><td>required</td><td>required</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:key</code></td><td>its time field</td><td>optional; default: the time field</td><td>optional; default: the time field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:from</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]])</td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:until</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]]), after <code>tss:from</code></td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasPoint</code></td><td>a point</td><td>not allowed</td><td>for a <code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code>: optional, any number; otherwise not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:points</code></td><td><code>rdf:JSON</code> literal</td><td>not allowed</td><td>for a <code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code>: required; otherwise not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>required</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:delimiter</code></td><td>one character</td><td>optional, for a <code>tss:CSVSource</code>; default <code>","</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:encoding</code></td><td>text, such as <code>"utf-8"</code></td><td>optional, for a <code>tss:CSVSource</code>; default <code>"utf-8"</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:header</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>optional, for a <code>tss:CSVSource</code>; default <code>true</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeBase</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]])</td><td>optional, together with <code>tss:timeStep</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeStep</code></td><td><code>xsd:duration</code>, more than zero</td><td>optional, together with <code>tss:timeBase</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:stepField</code></td><td>one of its fields</td><td>required with a time grid; not allowed without one</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:producedBy</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>rdfs:label</code></td><td>literal</td><td>optional, any number</td><td>optional, any number</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:sourceDescription</code></td><td>a node, any vocabulary</td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## Where the readings are ## {#storage}

The class of a source says where its points are and how to read them. `tss:Source` has five subclasses, the source classes:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Where the points are</th><th>Section</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code></td><td>in the metadata, as one JSON literal, <code>tss:points</code></td><td>[[#json-points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code></td><td>in the metadata, as RDF nodes that the source links to with <code>tss:hasPoint</code></td><td>[[#rdf-points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ParquetSource</code></td><td>in a Parquet data file, <code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>[[#parquet]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:CSVSource</code></td><td>in a CSV data file, <code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>[[#csv]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ArrowSource</code></td><td>in an Arrow data file, <code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>[[#arrow]]</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Every source MUST state exactly one source class as its `rdf:type`. A consumer selects the rules for a source by this one class: a source without a source class does not say where its readings are.

- An [=Embedded Source=] keeps its points in the metadata itself ([[#points]]). Every point states its own time and its readings themselves. The source MUST NOT state `tss:dataFile`, `tss:delimiter`, `tss:encoding` or `tss:header`, which describe a data file, nor `tss:timeBase`, `tss:timeStep` or `tss:stepField`, which describe a [=Time Grid=] ([[#time-grid]]). Its fields MUST NOT state a value encoding ([[#value-encoding]]). A `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` has no `tss:hasPoint`, and a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` has no `tss:points`.
- An [=External Source=] keeps its points in the data file `tss:dataFile`, read as its class says ([[#data-files]]). It has no `tss:hasPoint` and no `tss:points`.

All sources of a document MUST be embedded sources, or all MUST be external sources: a document never describes both.

## Time ## {#time}

### Times ### {#times}

A time of a source is a date and a time of day with a timezone, such as `"2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"`: an `xsd:dateTimeStamp` [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]], an `xsd:dateTime` whose timezone cannot be left out. The times of a source are:

- on the source: `tss:from`, `tss:until` and `tss:timeBase`;
- on a [=Stored Summary=]: `tss:firstTime`, `tss:lastTime` and `tss:generatedAtTime`;
- in each point: its value for the [=Time Field=].

The `tss:datatype` of the time field MUST be `xsd:dateTimeStamp`. A time stated in the metadata MUST be a literal with datatype `xsd:dateTimeStamp`. Every time MUST have a timezone: `Z` or an offset such as `+02:00`; a time without one is not valid. Times are compared as the moments they stand for: two times that are the same moment are the same time.

### The time field and the key ### {#time-and-key}

`tss:timeField` names the [=Time Field=]. It MUST be one of the source's own fields (`tss:hasField`). Every point MUST have a time: its value for the time field. In a data file with a [=Time Grid=], the time of a row is the time of its step ([[#time-grid]]).

`tss:key` names the field whose values identify a point: the [=Key=]. The key of a source is always its time field: a source states at most one `tss:key`, and when it states one, it MUST be its time field (`tss:timeField`). When a source does not state `tss:key`, its key is still its time field. No two points may have the same time; two times that are the same moment are the same time.

Note: The key has one field because a [=Field=] identifies its series: the sensor, the feature of interest and the observed property are stated once, on the field, not in a column of the points. A second key field, such as a station, would put several series in one field, and their observations and summaries could not be told apart. Several stations at the same time are written as one field per station, or as one source per station.

### The time window ### {#window}

`tss:from` and `tss:until` state the [=Time Window=] of the source: the readings lie in [`tss:from`, `tss:until`), so a point at exactly `tss:until` is not in the window. Both are times of the source ([[#times]]), and `tss:until` MUST come after `tss:from`. The time of every point of an embedded source MUST lie in the window. Consumers of an external source use only the rows inside the window: the data file may hold more.

Note: The window does not have to be tight: `tss:until` may lie after the newest point, as in <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>. The window of a [=Growing Source=] does not change ([[#change]]), so it is set to cover the readings that will still be added, such as a whole day, or left open.

## Changes ## {#change}

`tss:immutable` says whether readings may still be added to the source, as `ldes:immutable` does for a page of a Linked Data Event Stream:

- `true` means the source is final: no point is added, changed or removed, and nothing in its description changes any more.
- `false`, or no `tss:immutable`, means the source is a [=Growing Source=]: it is append-only. Points may be added ([[#updating]]), but no point is changed or removed.

A source that states `tss:immutable true` MUST NOT become a growing source again. A writer ends a growing source by adding `tss:immutable true` ([[#ending]]).

While a source is growing, its description does not change: a [=Write=] only adds points. Its class, its fields, its [=Time Window=], its data file and its [=Time Grid=] MUST stay as they are. The only statements that are added to its description are `tss:immutable true` and the [=Stored Summaries=] of its fields, at the moment the writer ends the source ([[#ending]]). Every point that is added MUST lie in the time window: a writer that has readings outside of it starts a new source.

A consumer learns that points were added by fetching the source again, and fetches only the new points with HTTP Range requests where the serialisation allows it ([[#following]]).

Some data files cannot be appended to, so their sources are always immutable: a `tss:ParquetSource` MUST state `tss:immutable true`, and so MUST a `tss:ArrowSource` whose data file is in the Arrow IPC file format ([[#arrow]]).

Note: Earlier drafts stated `tss:mutable` and `tss:committedUntil`. `tss:mutable true` is now the default, `tss:mutable false` is `tss:immutable true`, and a consumer finds out whether a growing source changed with HTTP validators (`ETag`, `Last-Modified`) instead of `tss:committedUntil` ([[#following]]).

## Description and provenance ## {#provenance}

- `tss:producedBy` is the agent that produced the data, as one IRI.
- `rdfs:label` names the source for people.
- `tss:sourceDescription` points to a node that describes the source in any vocabulary, for example where it is:

<div class="example" id="example-source-description">
```turtle
ex:source
    tss:sourceDescription [
        dcterms:spatial [
            a dcterms:Location ;
            rdfs:label "Site A"@en
        ]
    ] .
```
</div>

# Fields # {#fields}

Each [=Field=] is a `tss:Field`, linked from exactly one source with `tss:hasField`. The same field description reads a column of a data file, a key of the JSON points and the values of the RDF points. In an RDF point, the IRI of a field is the predicate of its values, so the fields of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` are IRIs and need no `tss:columnName`.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnName</code></td><td>non-empty text</td><td>required for a field of an external source, except the time field of a source with a [=Time Grid=], which has none; required for a field of a <code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code>; optional for a field of a <code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code></td><td>the name of the field's column (the CSV header, the Parquet or Arrow column), or its key in the JSON points; unique within the source</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:datatype</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>required</td><td>the datatype of the values, such as <code>xsd:double</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnIndex</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>optional; none for the time field of a source with a [=Time Grid=]</td><td>the position of the column, the first being 0; unique within the source</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:language</code></td><td>language tag</td><td>optional</td><td>the language of the values of an <code>rdf:langString</code> field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:minimum</code>, <code>tss:maximum</code></td><td>number</td><td>optional</td><td>the expected range of the [=Decoded Values=], bounds included; the maximum is not below the minimum. Informative: see [[#meaning]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:valueEncoding</code></td><td>IRI, such as <code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code></td><td>optional, for a field of an external source</td><td>how the values in the data file are stated when they are not the readings themselves ([[#value-encoding]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:baseline</code></td><td>number</td><td>with <code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code> only</td><td>the number that each value is the difference from ([[#value-encoding]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedProperty</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>what the values observe; makes the field a [=Measured Field=]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:unit</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>the unit of the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:sensor</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>the sensor that made the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:featureOfInterest</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>the thing the values are about</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:describesField</code></td><td>a field of the same source</td><td>optional, any number</td><td>the field whose values this field says something about</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:summary</code></td><td>a node</td><td>optional</td><td>a [=Stored Summary=] of the values ([[#summaries]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>rdfs:label</code>, <code>rdfs:comment</code></td><td>literal</td><td>optional, any number</td><td>for people</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:fieldDescription</code></td><td>a node, any vocabulary</td><td>optional</td><td>a free description of the field</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## Values and datatypes ## {#datatypes}

Every value is a text: a CSV cell, a JSON string of a JSON point, the lexical form of a literal in an RDF point, or a Parquet or Arrow value written in the lexical form of its type ([[#parquet]], [[#arrow]]). `tss:datatype` is the logical datatype that reads that text; the data file keeps its own types. In an RDF point, every value MUST be a literal whose datatype is the field's `tss:datatype`. A value MUST be in the lexical form of its datatype [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]]; an empty text is no value ([=Empty Value=]), so it is not checked. Implementations check at least these forms:

- `xsd:double`, `xsd:float` and `xsd:decimal`; `xsd:integer`, `xsd:long`, `xsd:int`, `xsd:short`, `xsd:byte`, `xsd:nonNegativeInteger`, `xsd:positiveInteger`, `xsd:nonPositiveInteger`, `xsd:negativeInteger` and the unsigned types;
- `xsd:boolean` (`true`, `false`, `1`, `0`);
- `xsd:dateTime`, `xsd:dateTimeStamp` and `xsd:date`, which must also be real days: 30 February is no date.

Any other datatype, such as `xsd:string`, accepts any text. A value keeps its text exactly as it is written: `" 12 "` is not read as 12, so a check can report it. The numeric datatypes are `xsd:double`, `xsd:float`, `xsd:decimal` and the XSD integer types: only a numeric field has an observed minimum and maximum, a mean and a median in its [=Stored Summary=].

An `rdf:langString` field states the language of its values with `tss:language`.

## What a field observes ## {#meaning}

`tss:observedProperty`, `tss:unit`, `tss:sensor` and `tss:featureOfInterest` say what the values of a field mean. They are stated field by field, so the fields of one source can observe different properties, with different sensors, in different units. When the source is expanded ([[#expanding]]), they are what each observation of the field holds.

`tss:minimum` and `tss:maximum` are the expected range of a numeric field, bounds included, and each of them applies on its own. They are not the range of the values that occur: those are in the stored summary (`tss:observedMinimum`, `tss:observedMaximum`). The range is informative: a value outside it does not make the source non-conforming, and the expansion keeps it. A consumer MAY use the range, for example to flag suspicious readings.

<div class="example" id="example-multi-sensor">
Several measured fields share one time field, each with its own property, unit and sensor. The point at 08:10 has no humidity: it has no triple for that field. The source states no `tss:key`, so its key is its time field.

```turtle
ex:weather-source
    a tss:EmbeddedRDFSource ;
    rdfs:label "Weather station readings"@en ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:until "2024-05-12T09:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:timeField ex:weather-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:weather-time-field,
        ex:temperature-field,
        ex:humidity-field .

ex:weather-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTimeStamp .

ex:temperature-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:Temperature ;
    tss:unit unit:DEG_C ;
    tss:sensor ex:thermometer ;
    tss:featureOfInterest ex:outside-air .

ex:humidity-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:minimum "0"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:maximum "100"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:RelativeHumidity ;
    tss:unit unit:PERCENT_RH ;
    tss:sensor ex:hygrometer ;
    tss:featureOfInterest ex:outside-air .

ex:weather-source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:weather-time-field "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:temperature-field "18.2"^^xsd:double ;
    ex:humidity-field "61.0"^^xsd:double
] .

ex:weather-source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:weather-time-field "2024-05-12T08:10:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:temperature-field "18.4"^^xsd:double
] .

ex:weather-source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:weather-time-field "2024-05-12T08:20:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:temperature-field "18.9"^^xsd:double ;
    ex:humidity-field "59.5"^^xsd:double
] .
```

Expanded ([[#expanding]]), the three points give five observations: the point at 08:10 only gives a temperature, as it has no humidity.
</div>

## Describing fields ## {#describing}

A [=Describing Field=] says something about the values of another field of the same source, in the same point. It states that field with `tss:describesField`.

<div class="example" id="example-describing-field">
Two series in one embedded source: a value, and a status that describes it.

```turtle
ex:two-series-source
    a tss:EmbeddedRDFSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:timeField ex:two-series-time-field ;
    tss:key ex:two-series-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:two-series-time-field,
        ex:two-series-value-field,
        ex:two-series-status-field .

ex:two-series-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTimeStamp .

ex:two-series-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    tss:unit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM .

ex:two-series-status-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:datatype xsd:string ;
    tss:describesField ex:two-series-value-field .

ex:two-series-source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:two-series-time-field "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:two-series-value-field "4810.5"^^xsd:double ;
    ex:two-series-status-field "ok"^^xsd:string
] .

ex:two-series-source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:two-series-time-field "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:two-series-value-field "4722.4"^^xsd:double ;
    ex:two-series-status-field "estimated"^^xsd:string
] .
```
</div>

A describing field with its own `tss:observedProperty` is also a [=Measured Field=]: its values become observations of their own.

`tss:fieldDescription`, like `tss:sourceDescription`, points to a node that describes the field in any vocabulary:

<div class="example" id="example-field-description">
```turtle
ex:value-field
    tss:fieldDescription [
        dcterms:description "Electrical conductivity"@en ;
        dcterms:subject quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
        dcterms:relation ex:sensor-1, ex:feature-1
    ] .
```
</div>

# Embedded points # {#points}

An [=Embedded Source=] keeps its points in the metadata. Its class says how they are written: as RDF nodes in a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` ([[#rdf-points]]), or as one JSON literal in a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` ([[#json-points]]). Either way:

- Every point has a value for the time field ([[#time-and-key]]), and states its readings themselves: an embedded source has no [=Time Grid=] and no value encoding.
- A point has at most one value for each field.
- The points have no order. A consumer orders them by their time.
- A source may have no points yet.

## RDF points ## {#rdf-points}

A `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` keeps its points as RDF. The source links to each [=Point=] with `tss:hasPoint`, and a point is a point of exactly one source. Every node that a source links to with `tss:hasPoint` is a point: its type follows from that link, so a point states no `rdf:type`. It needs no identifier either, so it is usually a blank node, written as one statement at the end of the document, as in <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>.

The metadata document of a [=Growing Source=] of this class MUST be an RDF Message Log [[RDF-MESSAGES]], such as Turtle or TriG that announces the version label `1.2-messages`. Its first message describes the sources and their fields, and every next message holds one or more points. A [=Write=] then adds a point by appending one message ([[#updating]]), and a consumer reads the new points without reading the document again ([[#following]]). The metadata document is read as the RDF merge of its messages: since blank node identifiers are scoped to the message they occur in, the blank nodes of two points are never the same node. A writer MUST end every message of the log of a growing source with a message delimiter (`MESSAGE`, or `@message .`), so a consumer can tell a complete message from one that is still being written. A delimiter right before the end of the document does not start a new message [[RDF-MESSAGES]].

- Each value of a point is one triple: the point is the subject, the IRI of the field the predicate, and the object a literal with the field's `tss:datatype` ([[#datatypes]]).
- The fields need no `tss:columnName`: the IRI of a field names its values. A field MAY state one, for example to name its column when the source is written to a data file; it has no effect on reading the RDF points.
- A point has no triple for a field it has no value for: that is an [=Empty Value=]. A value with the empty text `""` is no value either.
- Every value of a field of the source MUST be in a point of that source.
- Every property of a point SHOULD be a field of its source. Values of other properties are not values of the source.

No property of a point has a meaning of its own: the fields say what each value means.

## JSON points ## {#json-points}

A `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` MUST have exactly one `tss:points`: a literal with datatype `rdf:JSON` [[JSON-LD11]] whose value is a JSON array of JSON objects, one object per [=Point=]. The empty array `[]` holds no points.

- The keys of an object are the `tss:columnName` of the source's fields, so every field of a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` MUST state a `tss:columnName`. A point has no key for a field it has no value for.
- A value SHOULD be a JSON string that holds the lexical form of the field's datatype: `"2.50"`, not `2.50`. A JSON number loses its text when it is read: `2.50` may come back as `2.5` or as `2.5E0`.
- A missing key, `null` and `""` are an [=Empty Value=]: the point has no value for that field.
- A key MUST NOT start with `@`, as JSON-LD keywords such as `@context` and `@id` do.
- Every key SHOULD be the column name of a field. Values under other keys are not values of the source.

No key has a meaning of its own: the fields say what each key means. A point has no identifier.

<div class="example" id="example-json-points">
The readings of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a> as JSON points:

```turtle
ex:json-source
    a tss:EmbeddedJSONSource ;
    tss:immutable false ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:timeField ex:json-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:json-time-field,
        ex:json-value-field ;
    tss:points """[
        { "time": "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z", "value": "4810.5" },
        { "time": "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z", "value": "4722.4" },
        { "time": "2024-05-12T08:30:00Z", "value": "4751.9" },
        { "time": "2024-05-12T09:15:00Z", "value": "4719.3" },
        { "time": "2024-05-12T09:45:00Z", "value": "4783.6" }
      ]"""^^rdf:JSON .

ex:json-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTimeStamp .

ex:json-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The keys of the points are the fields' `tss:columnName`. The source states no `tss:key`, so its key is its time field.
</div>

# External data files # {#data-files}

An [=External Source=] keeps its points in the file `tss:dataFile`, an IRI that is resolved against the IRI of the metadata document, so it may be relative (`<data.csv>`). Its class says how to read the file: a `tss:CSVSource` as CSV ([[#csv]]), a `tss:ParquetSource` as Parquet ([[#parquet]]) and a `tss:ArrowSource` as Arrow ([[#arrow]]). The metadata does not change the file: the file keeps its own column names and types, and the fields say how to read them. Each row of the file is a [=Point=].

## CSV ## {#csv}

The data file of a `tss:CSVSource` is read as CSV [[RFC4180]]:

- `tss:delimiter` is the one character that separates the cells, such as `","` or `";"`. When it is not stated, it is `","`.
- `tss:encoding` is the text encoding of the file. When it is not stated, it is `"utf-8"`.
- `tss:header` says whether the first line names the columns. When it is not stated, the file has a header line.
- With a header line, a field's column is the column whose name is the field's `tss:columnName`. Without one (`tss:header false`), it is the column at the field's `tss:columnIndex`: the first column is 0.
- An empty cell is an [=Empty Value=].

<div class="example" id="example-csv-source">
An external source with a CSV file. It states its delimiter; the header line and the encoding are the defaults. It states no `tss:key`, so its key is its time field.

```turtle
ex:csv-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:from "2024-05-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/data.csv> ;
    tss:delimiter ";" ;
    tss:timeField ex:csv-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:csv-time-field,
        ex:csv-value-field .

ex:csv-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTimeStamp .

ex:csv-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The first lines of the data file:

```
time;value
2024-05-01T00:00:00Z;12.5
2024-05-01T00:15:00Z;12.7
```
</div>

<div class="example" id="example-csv-no-header">
An external source with a CSV file without a header line, and with an open time window: it states neither `tss:from` nor `tss:until`, so every row of the file is read. The columns are found by their `tss:columnIndex`.

```turtle
ex:headerless-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable false ;
    tss:dataFile <data-no-header.csv> ;
    tss:header false ;
    tss:timeField ex:headerless-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:headerless-time-field,
        ex:headerless-value-field .

ex:headerless-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:columnIndex 0 ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTimeStamp .

ex:headerless-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:columnIndex 1 ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The data file, relative to the metadata document:

```
2024-05-01T00:00:00Z,12.5
2024-05-01T00:15:00Z,12.7
```
</div>

## Parquet ## {#parquet}

The data file of a `tss:ParquetSource` is an [Apache Parquet](https://parquet.apache.org/docs/file-format/) file, read by its column names: a field's column is the column whose name is its `tss:columnName`. `tss:delimiter`, `tss:encoding` and `tss:header` have no effect on it. The values keep their Parquet types in the file. To check a value against the field's datatype, the value is written in the lexical form that the table below gives for the Parquet type of its column, and the field's `tss:datatype` MUST be one of the datatypes the table allows for that type. A Parquet NULL is an [=Empty Value=]. A column of a Parquet type that is not in the table is not supported by this specification.

A Parquet file ends with a footer that indexes its row groups, so rows cannot be appended to it. A `tss:ParquetSource` is therefore never a [=Growing Source=]: it MUST state `tss:immutable true`. A producer that keeps adding readings uses a CSV source or an Arrow source in the IPC streaming format instead, and can publish the readings as a Parquet source once no readings are added any more.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Parquet type</th><th>Lexical form</th><th>Allowed <code>tss:datatype</code></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>BOOLEAN</code></td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>INT32</code>, <code>INT64</code>, <code>INT(8, 16, 32 or 64, signed)</code></td><td>decimal digits, with a <code>-</code> sign when negative</td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, or the XSD integer type whose range holds the values, such as <code>xsd:int</code> or <code>xsd:long</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>INT(8, 16, 32 or 64, unsigned)</code></td><td>decimal digits</td><td><code>xsd:nonNegativeInteger</code>, or an unsigned XSD type such as <code>xsd:unsignedInt</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>FLOAT</code></td><td>the XSD lexical form of the value, with <code>INF</code>, <code>-INF</code> and <code>NaN</code> for the special values</td><td><code>xsd:float</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DOUBLE</code></td><td>the same</td><td><code>xsd:double</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DECIMAL(precision, scale)</code></td><td>decimal digits with a <code>.</code> and exactly <em>scale</em> fraction digits</td><td><code>xsd:decimal</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>STRING</code>, <code>ENUM</code></td><td>the text</td><td><code>xsd:string</code>, or any other datatype that reads a text</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DATE</code></td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code></td><td><code>xsd:date</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TIMESTAMP(isAdjustedToUTC = true)</code>, in milli-, micro- or nanoseconds</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.fraction]Z</code>, in UTC, with a fraction only when the value has one</td><td><code>xsd:dateTimeStamp</code>, or <code>xsd:dateTime</code> for a field that is not the time field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TIMESTAMP(isAdjustedToUTC = false)</code></td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.fraction]</code>, without timezone, so it cannot be the column of the time field ([[#times]])</td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TIME</code></td><td><code>hh:mm:ss[.fraction]</code></td><td><code>xsd:time</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<div class="example" id="example-parquet-source">
The class and the data file of a Parquet source:

```turtle
ex:parquet-source
    a tss:ParquetSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/data.parquet> .
```
</div>

## Arrow ## {#arrow}

The data file of a `tss:ArrowSource` is written in one of the two IPC formats of Apache Arrow [[ARROW-IPC]]:

- the [IPC streaming format](https://arrow.apache.org/docs/format/Columnar.html#ipc-streaming-format): a schema message, followed by dictionary batches and record batches, and optionally an end-of-stream marker. A stream can be appended to, so it is the format of a [=Growing Source=]. Its media type is `application/vnd.apache.arrow.stream`, and its file extension is usually `.arrows`.
- the [IPC file format](https://arrow.apache.org/docs/format/Columnar.html#ipc-file-format): the same messages between the magic string `ARROW1` at the start and a footer at the end that indexes the record batches. Every append would rewrite the footer, so a source in the file format MUST state `tss:immutable true`. Its media type is `application/vnd.apache.arrow.file`, and its file extension is usually `.arrow`.

The data file of a growing `tss:ArrowSource` MUST be in the IPC streaming format. The data file of an immutable one MAY be in either format. A consumer tells them apart by the first bytes of the file, not by its extension or media type: a file in the IPC file format starts with the 6 bytes `ARROW1`, a stream starts with the continuation marker `0xFFFFFFFF`. A server SHOULD serve the data file with the media type of its format.

The data file is read by its column names, as a Parquet file is: a field's column is the column whose name is its `tss:columnName`. `tss:delimiter`, `tss:encoding` and `tss:header` have no effect on it. The values keep their Arrow types in the file, and a dictionary-encoded column is read as the values it encodes. To check a value against the field's datatype, the value is written in the lexical form that the table below gives for the Arrow type of its column, and the field's `tss:datatype` MUST be one of the datatypes the table allows for that type. An Arrow null is an [=Empty Value=]. A column of an Arrow type that is not in the table is not supported by this specification.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Arrow type</th><th>Lexical form</th><th>Allowed <code>tss:datatype</code></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>Bool</code></td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Int</code> (8, 16, 32 or 64 bits, signed)</td><td>decimal digits, with a <code>-</code> sign when negative</td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, or the XSD integer type whose range holds the values, such as <code>xsd:int</code> or <code>xsd:long</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Int</code> (8, 16, 32 or 64 bits, unsigned)</td><td>decimal digits</td><td><code>xsd:nonNegativeInteger</code>, or an unsigned XSD type such as <code>xsd:unsignedInt</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>FloatingPoint</code> (half or single precision)</td><td>the XSD lexical form of the value, with <code>INF</code>, <code>-INF</code> and <code>NaN</code> for the special values</td><td><code>xsd:float</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>FloatingPoint</code> (double precision)</td><td>the same</td><td><code>xsd:double</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Decimal</code>(precision, scale)</td><td>decimal digits with a <code>.</code> and exactly <em>scale</em> fraction digits</td><td><code>xsd:decimal</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Utf8</code>, <code>LargeUtf8</code>, <code>Utf8View</code></td><td>the text</td><td><code>xsd:string</code>, or any other datatype that reads a text</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Date</code> (in days or in milliseconds)</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code></td><td><code>xsd:date</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Timestamp</code> with a timezone, in seconds, milli-, micro- or nanoseconds</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.fraction]Z</code>, in UTC, with a fraction only when the value has one</td><td><code>xsd:dateTimeStamp</code>, or <code>xsd:dateTime</code> for a field that is not the time field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Timestamp</code> without a timezone</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.fraction]</code>, without timezone, so it cannot be the column of the time field ([[#times]])</td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Time</code></td><td><code>hh:mm:ss[.fraction]</code></td><td><code>xsd:time</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<div class="example" id="example-arrow-source">
The class and the data file of two Arrow sources: an immutable one in the IPC file format, and a growing one in the IPC streaming format, to which record batches are still appended ([[#appending-data-files]]).

```turtle
ex:arrow-source
    a tss:ArrowSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/data.arrow> .

ex:arrow-stream-source
    a tss:ArrowSource ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/live.arrows> .
```
</div>

## Time grid ## {#time-grid}

An external source MAY give the time of each row as a step on a [=Time Grid=], instead of in a column of its own. It states the grid with `tss:timeBase`, the time of step 0, and `tss:timeStep`, the time between two steps, and MUST state both or neither. An embedded source MUST NOT state a time grid: every point states its own time.

- `tss:timeBase` is a time of the source ([[#times]]). It anchors the grid and is not a bound of the [=Time Window=]: it stays the same when `tss:from` or `tss:until` change, so no row's time moves.
- `tss:timeStep` is an `xsd:duration` [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]] of more than zero, such as `"PT15M"` or `"P1M"`.

Step n is at `tss:timeBase` + n × `tss:timeStep`: each part of the step (years, months, days, hours, minutes and seconds) is multiplied by n, and the result is added to `tss:timeBase` as XML Schema adds a duration to a date and time [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]].

Note: With years or months, the steps are calendar steps, not equal lengths of time: a month has 28 to 31 days, and a day past the end of a month becomes its last day (2021-01-31 + P1M is 2021-02-28).

With a time grid, a column of the data file holds the step of each row. The source names the field of that column, the [=Step Field=], with exactly one `tss:stepField`, as `tss:timeField` names the time field:

- The step field MUST be one of the source's own fields (`tss:hasField`), and MUST NOT be its time field. Its `tss:datatype` MUST be `xsd:integer`.
- Every row MUST have a step: a whole number of 0 or more. No two rows may be at the same step.
- A row's time is the time of its step: that is the row's value for the time field. The data file has no column for the time field, so the time field MUST NOT state a `tss:columnName` or a `tss:columnIndex`.

A source without a time grid MUST NOT state a `tss:stepField`.

<div class="example" id="example-time-grid">
The readings of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a> in a CSV file, on a time grid of 15 minutes from 08:00. The column `step` holds the step of each row; the time field has no column.

```turtle
ex:grid-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/grid.csv> ;
    tss:timeBase "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    tss:timeStep "PT15M"^^xsd:duration ;
    tss:timeField ex:grid-time-field ;
    tss:stepField ex:grid-step-field ;
    tss:key ex:grid-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:grid-time-field,
        ex:grid-step-field,
        ex:grid-value-field .

ex:grid-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTimeStamp .

ex:grid-step-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "step" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:integer .

ex:grid-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The data file:

```
step,value
0,4810.5
1,4722.4
2,4751.9
5,4719.3
7,4783.6
```

Steps 3, 4 and 6 have no row. Step 5 is at 2024-05-12T08:00:00Z + 5 × PT15M, which is 2024-05-12T09:15:00Z.
</div>

## Value encoding ## {#value-encoding}

A field of an external source MAY state with `tss:valueEncoding` that the values in the data file are not the readings themselves. A field without a `tss:valueEncoding` states its readings: each value is its own [=Decoded Value=]. A field has at most one `tss:valueEncoding`, and the time field has none. The fields of an embedded source MUST NOT state `tss:valueEncoding` or `tss:baseline`: every point states the readings themselves.

This specification defines one value encoding, `tss:DeltaFromBaseline`: each value is the difference from the field's `tss:baseline`, and its decoded value is `tss:baseline` + the value. The sum is computed exactly on the decimal values of the two literals, and then read as the field's `tss:datatype`. The differences may be negative.

- A field with `tss:DeltaFromBaseline` MUST state exactly one `tss:baseline`: a number (`xsd:integer`, `xsd:decimal`, `xsd:double` or `xsd:float`) with the field's `tss:datatype`.
- A field without `tss:DeltaFromBaseline` MUST NOT state a `tss:baseline`.

`tss:minimum`, `tss:maximum` and the [=Stored Summary=] of the field describe the decoded values, not the differences, and so do the observations of the field ([[#expanding]]).

<div class="example" id="example-delta-from-baseline">
The first two readings of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a> in a CSV file whose value column states the difference from a baseline of 4750.

```turtle
ex:delta-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/delta.csv> ;
    tss:timeField ex:delta-time-field ;
    tss:key ex:delta-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:delta-time-field,
        ex:delta-value-field .

ex:delta-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTimeStamp .

ex:delta-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:valueEncoding tss:DeltaFromBaseline ;
    tss:baseline "4750"^^xsd:double .
```

The data file:

```
time,value
2024-05-12T08:00:00Z,60.5
2024-05-12T08:15:00Z,-27.6
```

Their decoded values are 4750 + 60.5 = 4810.5 and 4750 + (-27.6) = 4722.4.
</div>

# Stored summaries # {#summaries}

A field of an immutable source MAY have one `tss:summary`: a node that holds numbers computed from the values of the field, such as how many values there are and their mean. A consumer can use them without reading the data. Every attribute of a summary is optional, and a summary may be empty.

A field of a [=Growing Source=] MUST NOT have a `tss:summary`: its numbers would be outdated by the next [=Write=], and the description of a growing source does not change ([[#change]]). A writer adds the summaries when it ends the source ([[#ending]]).

The numbers of a summary cover the points in the [=Time Window=] of the source: from its `tss:from` (included) until its `tss:until` (not included), with an open side when the source leaves it open. A summary has no window of its own. They are computed from the [=Decoded Values=] of the field ([[#value-encoding]]).

A summary states with `tss:missingValuePolicy` which values its numbers leave out. This specification defines one such policy, `tss:ExcludeEmptyValues`: an [=Empty Value=] is not a value, so the numbers leave it out. A text of only spaces is a value.

The attributes of a summary:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>which values the numbers leave out, such as <code>tss:ExcludeEmptyValues</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:count</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>how many points in the window have a value for the field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:missing</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>how many points in the window have no value for the field; a time without a point is not counted</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:firstTime</code>, <code>tss:lastTime</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]])</td><td>the time of the first and of the last point that has a value</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedMinimum</code>, <code>tss:observedMaximum</code></td><td>number</td><td>the smallest and the largest value</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:mean</code></td><td>number</td><td>the unweighted arithmetic mean of the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:median</code></td><td>number</td><td>the middle value, or the mean of the two middle values when their number is even</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:generatedAtTime</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]])</td><td>when the numbers were computed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Only a numeric field ([[#datatypes]]) has `tss:observedMinimum`, `tss:observedMaximum`, `tss:mean` and `tss:median`. They include every value in the window, also values outside the expected range of the field: the observed minimum and maximum do not replace `tss:minimum` and `tss:maximum` ([[#meaning]]).

<div class="example" id="example-summary">
The summary of the value field of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>, which the writer adds when it ends the source after its fifth point ([[#ending]]):

```turtle
ex:value-field
    tss:summary [
        tss:missingValuePolicy tss:ExcludeEmptyValues ;
        tss:count "5"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:missing "0"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:firstTime "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
        tss:lastTime "2024-05-12T09:45:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
        tss:observedMinimum "4719.3"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:observedMaximum "4810.5"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:mean "4757.54"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:median "4751.9"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:generatedAtTime "2024-05-12T09:46:10Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp
    ] .
```
</div>

<div class="example" id="example-summary-missing">
The summary of the humidity field of <a href="#example-multi-sensor">the multi-sensor example</a>. One of the three points has no humidity: it is counted in `tss:missing`, and the numbers leave it out.

```turtle
ex:humidity-field
    tss:summary [
        tss:missingValuePolicy tss:ExcludeEmptyValues ;
        tss:count "2"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:missing "1"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:firstTime "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
        tss:lastTime "2024-05-12T08:20:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
        tss:observedMinimum "59.5"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:observedMaximum "61.0"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:mean "60.25"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:median "60.25"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:generatedAtTime "2024-05-12T09:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp
    ] .
```
</div>

A summary is computed once, from the final points of the source. Its `tss:generatedAtTime` says when it was computed, and a consumer can compute the same numbers from the data to check it.

# Expanding a source into observations # {#expanding}

A source can be expanded into observations: every value of a [=Measured Field=] is one observation. An observation holds:

- the source: a link back to the `tss:Source` it comes from;
- the observed property: the field's `tss:observedProperty`;
- the sensor, the feature of interest and the unit: the field's `tss:sensor`, `tss:featureOfInterest` and `tss:unit`, when stated;
- the time: the time of its point, which in a data file with a [=Time Grid=] is the time of its step;
- the result: the [=Decoded Value=] of the value, as a literal with the field's `tss:datatype`, or with its `tss:language` for `rdf:langString`; a value without a value encoding keeps its text as written.

To expand a source:

1. Read the points: from the data file as [[#data-files]] says, or from the metadata as [[#points]] says.
2. Keep the points whose time lies in the [=Time Window=]. A point without a time is an error.
3. For each point, and for each measured field for which the point has a value, make one observation: a new blank node that links back to the source.
4. A value that is not in the lexical form of its datatype is an error.
5. Add what the metadata says about the IRIs that the observations use, such as their observed properties and sensors: the concise bounded description of each, without the statements that use the TSS vocabulary.

Which RDF vocabulary the observations are written with is left to the implementer. The output holds no TSS vocabulary, and no description of the source or its fields: an observation only links back to its source. <a href="#example-observations">The observations of the embedded example</a> show the result for two points.

A document with several sources is expanded source by source: the observations of a source only link back to that source.

Note: The expansion does not keep every value: a field without `tss:observedProperty`, such as an identifier or a status, gives no observations.

## Mapping to SOSA (informative) ## {#sosa-mapping}

*This section is informative.* An implementation that has no reason to choose another vocabulary can write the observations with SOSA [[VOCAB-SSN]], QUDT and Dublin Core, as in <a href="#example-observations">the example</a>:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Part of an observation</th><th>Written as</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>the observation</td><td>a blank node of type <code>sosa:Observation</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the source</td><td><code>dcterms:source</code>, with the IRI of the <code>tss:Source</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the observed property</td><td><code>sosa:observedProperty</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the sensor</td><td><code>sosa:madeBySensor</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the feature of interest</td><td><code>sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the unit</td><td><code>qudt:hasUnit</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the time of the point</td><td><code>sosa:resultTime</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the result</td><td><code>sosa:hasSimpleResult</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

# Adding points to a growing source # {#updating}

A [=Growing Source=] is append-only: points are added, and an existing point is never changed or removed. Every serialisation of its points only grows. A representation that a server returned, without content coding, MUST be a byte prefix of every later representation of the same resource in the same media type, as long as the resource is served at that IRI:

- the metadata document of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, an RDF Message Log ([[#rdf-points]]);
- the data file of a `tss:CSVSource` ([[#appending-data-files]]);
- the data file of a `tss:ArrowSource`, in the IPC streaming format ([[#arrow]]).

The `tss:points` literal of a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` is rewritten on every [=Write=], so its document does not only grow; its points do ([[#updating-embedded]]). A producer that has to rewrite earlier bytes, for example to compact or correct the data, publishes the result as a new source, under a new IRI.

## Embedded sources ## {#updating-embedded}

A [=Write=] that adds one point to a growing [=Embedded Source=]:

1. Checks the new point: every value is in the lexical form of its field's datatype, no other point has the same time (the [=Key=]), and its time lies in the [=Time Window=] of the source.
2. Adds the new point, and leaves everything else as it is. In a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, it appends one RDF Message to the metadata document: one statement that links the source to a new node with `tss:hasPoint`, with one triple for each of its values, followed by a message delimiter. In a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource`, it writes a new `tss:points`: the existing points, unchanged and in the same order, followed by the new one as a JSON object whose values are JSON strings.
3. Keeps the change only when the source still meets this specification.

A Write that adds several points at once does the same for each of them; in a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, it MAY put them in one message. A Write changes nothing else: not the time window, and not the description of the source or its fields ([[#change]]).

<div class="example" id="example-write">
A new point for <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>, appended to its RDF Message Log:

```turtle
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T10:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTimeStamp ;
    ex:value-field "4766.2"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
```

The new point lies in the window, which stays as it is. In <a href="#example-json-points">the JSON points</a>, the same point is the object `{ "time": "2024-05-12T10:00:00Z", "value": "4766.2" }`, added at the end of the array in `tss:points`.
</div>

## Data files ## {#appending-data-files}

The data file of a growing [=External Source=] only grows: rows are appended to it, and no row is changed or removed. How a producer writes to the file is outside this specification, but the file MUST meet these rules after every append, so a consumer can read the new rows on their own ([[#following]]):

- **CSV:** a writer appends complete records only, and ends every record with a line break, so the file always ends with a line break. The header line and the earlier records MUST NOT change. A record that is not followed by a line break yet, is still being written. The data file of a growing CSV source SHOULD NOT be compressed as a whole, such as in a `.csv.gz` file, because then the new rows cannot be read on their own; a server MAY still compress it with an HTTP content coding.
- **Arrow:** the data file is in the IPC streaming format ([[#arrow]]). A writer appends complete encapsulated messages only: record batches, and dictionary batches, including delta dictionary batches, when a dictionary-encoded column needs new values. The schema message at the start of the stream MUST NOT change. A writer MUST NOT write the end-of-stream marker while the source is growing.
- **Parquet**, and Arrow in the IPC file format, cannot be appended to: such a source is never growing ([[#parquet]], [[#arrow]]).

The time of a new row MUST lie in the [=Time Window=] of the source. On a [=Time Grid=], `tss:timeBase` and `tss:timeStep` do not change, so new rows continue the steps of the grid.

## Ending a growing source ## {#ending}

A writer ends a growing source when no point will be added to it any more. It then:

1. Writes the last points: the data file of an external source holds all its rows before the next step.
2. Adds `tss:immutable true` to the description of the source, and MAY add a [=Stored Summary=] to each field ([[#summaries]]). In the RDF Message Log of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, it appends one last message that holds these statements. In the metadata document of any other source, it adds them to the description.
3. In the IPC streaming format of an Arrow data file, MAY append the end-of-stream marker.

<div class="example" id="example-ending">
The last message of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>, which ends the source and adds the summary of its value field (<a href="#example-summary">the summary example</a>):

```turtle
ex:source tss:immutable true .

ex:value-field tss:summary [
    tss:missingValuePolicy tss:ExcludeEmptyValues ;
    tss:count "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    # ... and the other numbers of the summary
] .
MESSAGE
```
</div>

The RDF Message Log of a growing source MUST NOT state `tss:immutable false`: the merge of all its messages would then state both `false` and `true`. Without it, the merge stays a conforming metadata document: it only gains `tss:immutable true` and the summaries. In a metadata document that is not a log, a writer replaces `tss:immutable false`, if stated, by `tss:immutable true`.

A consumer that reads `tss:immutable true` reads the rest of the points once more, and then stops following the source ([[#following]]).

# Following a growing source # {#following}

A consumer that follows a [=Growing Source=] reads its points, and later reads the points that were added since. This section says how it finds out that points were added, and how it fetches only the new ones over HTTP [[RFC9110]]. A source usually holds a whole time series ([[#intro]]). When a producer still spreads a series over several sources, finding them and moving on to the next one are left to a collection that contains them, such as the pages of a [Linked Data Event Stream](https://w3id.org/ldes/specification).

## Polling and caching ## {#polling}

A publisher of a growing source SHOULD send an `ETag` or a `Last-Modified` header with its metadata document and with its data file, and SHOULD answer conditional requests (`If-None-Match`, `If-Modified-Since`) with `304 Not Modified` when nothing changed. It SHOULD state with `Cache-Control: max-age` how long a consumer waits before it asks again, and a consumer SHOULD NOT ask more often. Once a source is immutable, its publisher SHOULD send `Cache-Control: immutable` with a long `max-age` for its metadata document and its data file.

A consumer of an [=External Source=] polls its metadata document and its data file each on its own. When the metadata document states `tss:immutable true`, all rows are in the data file ([[#ending]]): the consumer reads the data file once more, and stops.

## Reading only the new bytes ## {#range-requests}

The RDF Message Log of a growing `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, and the data file of a growing `tss:CSVSource` or `tss:ArrowSource`, only grow ([[#updating]]). A consumer then does not download them again, but asks for the bytes after the ones it has read, with a Range request [[RFC9110]]. It reads them in units that a writer appends whole:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Source</th><th>Unit</th><th>A unit is complete when</th><th>State to keep</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code></td><td>an RDF Message [[RDF-MESSAGES]]</td><td>a message delimiter follows it</td><td>the version label, the prefixes and the base IRI that are in force at the end of the last complete message</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:CSVSource</code></td><td>a CSV record [[RFC4180]]</td><td>the line break that ends it follows; a line break inside a quoted cell does not end a record</td><td>the header line, and so the columns of the fields</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ArrowSource</code></td><td>an encapsulated message of the IPC streaming format [[ARROW-IPC]]</td><td>all its bytes are there: the continuation marker, the length of its metadata, the metadata, and a body of the length that the metadata states</td><td>the schema, and the dictionaries so far</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

A consumer that follows such a resource:

1. Reads the whole representation once, with a request without `Range`. It keeps the state of the table, and the offset *N*: the number of bytes up to the end of the last complete unit. A last unit that is not complete is not read yet.
2. Asks for the rest with `Range: bytes=N-`. It does not send `If-Range`: every append changes the validators of the resource, so `If-Range` would return the whole representation again. Byte offsets count the bytes of the representation without content coding, so the consumer SHOULD send `Accept-Encoding: identity` with a Range request.
3. Handles the answer:
    - `206 Partial Content` with a range that starts at *N*: it reads the new bytes with the state it kept, emits the points of the complete units, and moves *N* to the end of the last complete unit.
    - `416 Range Not Satisfiable` with `Content-Range: bytes */L`: when *L* is *N*, nothing was added. When *L* is less than *N*, the resource shrank, and the consumer falls back (step 4).
    - `200 OK`: the server ignored the Range header and returned the whole representation. The consumer falls back (step 4) on that representation.
    - `410 Gone` or `404 Not Found`: the source is no longer served at this IRI; the consumer stops.
4. Falls back when the new bytes cannot be read as expected: when they do not start with a complete unit, do not parse with the state it kept, or when the resource shrank. It drops its state, and reads the whole representation again from byte 0, as in step 1. It does not emit again a point whose time it has emitted already: the [=Key=] identifies a point.
5. Repeats from step 2, as often as the caching rules allow ([[#polling]]), until the source is immutable: it states `tss:immutable true`, or, for an Arrow stream, the end-of-stream marker follows the last complete unit.

A consumer MAY check that the bytes it read before did not change, by asking for `Range: bytes=(N-k)-` and comparing the first *k* bytes with the last *k* bytes it read. When they differ, it falls back (step 4). A server and a consumer MAY use an open-ended live range [[RFC8673]] to receive the new bytes as they are appended, without polling.

## Embedded JSON points ## {#following-json}

The metadata document of a growing `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` is not append-only: a [=Write=] rewrites `tss:points`. A consumer that follows it asks for the whole document again with a conditional request ([[#polling]]). The existing objects of `tss:points` do not change and stay in the same order ([[#updating-embedded]]): when the consumer has read *n* objects, it emits the objects after the first *n*. When the first *n* objects are not the ones it read, it falls back as in [[#range-requests]], step 4.

# Vocabulary overview # {#vocabulary}

*This section is informative.* All terms are in the namespace `https://w3id.org/tss#`.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Kind</th><th>Used on</th><th>Section</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:Source</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#the-source]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code>, <code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code>, <code>tss:ParquetSource</code>, <code>tss:CSVSource</code>, <code>tss:ArrowSource</code></td><td>class, subclass of <code>tss:Source</code></td><td>-</td><td>[[#storage]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Field</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#fields]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Point</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Summary</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:MissingValuePolicy</code>, <code>tss:ValueEncoding</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#summaries]], [[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ExcludeEmptyValues</code></td><td>individual</td><td>value of <code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code></td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code></td><td>individual</td><td>value of <code>tss:valueEncoding</code></td><td>[[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:immutable</code></td><td>property</td><td>source</td><td>[[#change]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasField</code>, <code>tss:timeField</code>, <code>tss:key</code></td><td>property</td><td>source</td><td>[[#time-and-key]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:from</code>, <code>tss:until</code></td><td>property</td><td>source</td><td>[[#window]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasPoint</code></td><td>property</td><td><code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code></td><td>[[#rdf-points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:points</code></td><td>property</td><td><code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code></td><td>[[#json-points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>property</td><td>external source</td><td>[[#data-files]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:delimiter</code>, <code>tss:header</code>, <code>tss:encoding</code></td><td>property</td><td><code>tss:CSVSource</code></td><td>[[#csv]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeBase</code>, <code>tss:timeStep</code>, <code>tss:stepField</code></td><td>property</td><td>external source</td><td>[[#time-grid]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:producedBy</code>, <code>tss:sourceDescription</code></td><td>property</td><td>source</td><td>[[#provenance]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnName</code>, <code>tss:columnIndex</code>, <code>tss:datatype</code>, <code>tss:language</code>, <code>tss:minimum</code>, <code>tss:maximum</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#fields]], [[#datatypes]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:valueEncoding</code>, <code>tss:baseline</code></td><td>property</td><td>field of an external source</td><td>[[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedProperty</code>, <code>tss:unit</code>, <code>tss:sensor</code>, <code>tss:featureOfInterest</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#meaning]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:describesField</code>, <code>tss:fieldDescription</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#describing]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:summary</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code>, <code>tss:count</code>, <code>tss:missing</code>, <code>tss:firstTime</code>, <code>tss:lastTime</code>, <code>tss:observedMinimum</code>, <code>tss:observedMaximum</code>, <code>tss:mean</code>, <code>tss:median</code>, <code>tss:generatedAtTime</code></td><td>property</td><td>summary</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The vocabulary reuses `rdfs:label`, `rdfs:comment`, the XML Schema datatypes [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]] and the datatype `rdf:JSON` [[JSON-LD11]].

<h2 class="no-num" id="conformance">Conformance</h2>

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "SHOULD" and "MAY" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [[!RFC2119]] [[!RFC8174]] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals.

Everything in this specification is normative, except the sections marked as informative, the examples, the notes and the issues.

This specification defines four conformance classes:

: <dfn export>Conforming metadata document</dfn>
:: An RDF document that describes at least one source and meets every requirement in this specification on a metadata document, a source, a field, embedded points and a stored summary. For an external source, this includes the requirements on the properties that describe the data file, its [=Time Grid=] and its value encoding, but not on the contents of the data file.
: <dfn export>Conforming data</dfn>
:: The points of a source, embedded or in its data file, that meet the requirements on points: the time of every point (on a [=Time Grid=], its step), the key, the lexical form of the values, and the [=Time Window=]. The points of an embedded source are part of its metadata document; the rows of a data file are not.
: <dfn export>Conforming consumer</dfn>
:: Software that reads a conforming metadata document and its data as this specification says: it selects the rules for each source by its class, reads the points as [[#points]] or [[#data-files]] says, reads the times as [[#times]] says, decodes the values of a field with a value encoding ([[#value-encoding]]), applies the [=Time Window=] and the [=Empty Value=] rules, and reports an error where this specification calls something an error. When it follows a [=Growing Source=], it does so as [[#following]] says. A <dfn export>conforming expander</dfn> is a conforming consumer that also expands a source into observations as [[#expanding]] says.
: <dfn export>Conforming writer</dfn>
:: Software that changes the points of an [=Embedded Source=] only by the [=Write=] of [[#updating-embedded]], that only appends to the data file of a [=Growing Source=] as [[#appending-data-files]] says, that ends a growing source as [[#ending]] says, and that leaves a conforming metadata document conforming after every change.
